WEBVTT

1
00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:34.208


2
00:00:34.208 --> 00:00:38.791
<b>Krishna's words to Arjun were sharp as a blade.</b>

3
00:00:38.791 --> 00:00:42.666
<b>He compared Arjun's thoughts to impotence</b>

4
00:00:42.666 --> 00:00:44.916
<b>and called his mind weak.</b>

5
00:00:44.916 --> 00:00:48.833
<b>Krishna's aim was to light a fire under Arjun,</b>

6
00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:50.750
<b>to wake him from his slumber.</b>

7
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.916
<b>And it's starting to work. </b>

8
00:00:53.916 --> 00:00:54.833
<b>Here,</b>

9
00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:58.083
<b>Arjun, stirred up, says to Krishna:</b>

10
00:00:58.083 --> 00:00:59.416
<b>"O Slayer of Enemies, </b>

11
00:00:59.416 --> 00:01:01.458
<b>O Vanquisher of Madhu,</b>

12
00:01:01.458 --> 00:01:02.583
<b>should I</b>

13
00:01:02.583 --> 00:01:09.916
<b>really unleash my arrows against my venerable</b>
<b>grandfather and my respected teacher Drona?"</b>

14
00:01:10.541 --> 00:01:14.750


15
00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.250
<b>Arjuna's getting worked up now.</b>

16
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:22.791
<b>He's addressing Krishna as</b>
<b>"Arisudana" and "Madhusudana" </b>

17
00:01:22.791 --> 00:01:28.833
<b>almost as if he's gently teasing</b>
<b>or reproaching Krishna.</b>

18
00:01:28.833 --> 00:01:32.250
<b>It's like he's saying, "You're Arisudana,</b>
<b>you're even Madhusudana </b>

19
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.083
<b>the one who slayed the demon Madhu.</b>

20
00:01:35.083 --> 00:01:38.875
<b>You've only killed demons and enemies,</b>

21
00:01:38.875 --> 00:01:42.333
<b>so why expect me to slay my own kin?"</b>

22
00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:46.875
<b>It's as if there's an imaginary debate going</b>
<b>on between Krishna and Arjuna.</b>

23
00:01:46.875 --> 00:01:49.208
<b>The words might go something like this,</b>

24
00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:50.666
<b>with Arjuna saying: </b>

25
00:01:50.666 --> 00:01:55.708
<b>"Krishna, how can anyone</b>
<b>fight their revered elders?"</b>

26
00:01:55.708 --> 00:01:56.583
<b>“Arjuna,</b>

27
00:01:56.583 --> 00:02:01.291
<b>but what if they're ready to fight you?</b>
<b>What's the problem then?”</b>

28
00:02:01.291 --> 00:02:02.291
<b>“Madhava,</b>

29
00:02:02.291 --> 00:02:08.458
<b>even if they want to, they're</b>
<b>still worthy of my respect.</b>

30
00:02:08.458 --> 00:02:14.625
<b>You've only fought demons and enemies.”</b>

31
00:02:14.625 --> 00:02:15.625
<b>“Hmm.</b>

32
00:02:15.625 --> 00:02:17.500
<b>But you're a warrior.</b>

33
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.625
<b>Do you know your duty in war?</b>

34
00:02:20.625 --> 00:02:22.583
<b>What about that duty?”</b>

35
00:02:22.583 --> 00:02:27.333
<b>This back-and-forth will become clearer</b>
<b>in the following verses.</b>

36
00:02:27.333 --> 00:02:28.750
<b>But there's one thing</b>

37
00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:30.625
<b>by calling Krishna "Arisudana"</b>

38
00:02:30.625 --> 00:02:35.000
<b>and "Madhusudana", </b>
<b>Arjuna's making a subtle jab</b>

39
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.000
<b>It shows he's agitated,</b>

40
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.625
<b>and even in this state, he's forgetting</b>

41
00:02:40.625 --> 00:02:43.541
<b>that when it came to dharma and truth,</b>

42
00:02:43.541 --> 00:02:48.333
<b>Krishna didn't hesitate to</b>
<b>slay his own uncle, Kansa.</b>

43
00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:50.416
<b>For Lord Krishna,</b>

44
00:02:50.416 --> 00:02:53.583
<b>dharma is above all else.</b>

45
00:02:54.208 --> 00:02:58.333


46
00:02:58.333 --> 00:03:07.916
<b>When someone questions your</b>
<b>intentions or doubts your abilities,</b>

47
00:03:07.916 --> 00:03:12.291
<b>don't you get riled up too?</b>

48
00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:21.833
<b>Don't you ready your sharp arguments</b>
<b>and counter-attacks, like Arjuna?</b>

49
00:03:21.833 --> 00:03:26.583
<b>It happens, doesn't it?</b>

50
00:03:26.583 --> 00:03:28.583
<b>Especially when</b>

51
00:03:28.583 --> 00:03:34.500
<b> the doubter is someone close,</b>

52
00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:37.000
<b>someone dear.</b>

53
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.583
<b>Like I am</b>

54
00:03:39.583 --> 00:03:41.291
<b> to Arjuna.</b>

55
00:03:41.291 --> 00:03:47.333
<b>Arjuna feels I'm misunderstanding him.</b>

56
00:03:47.333 --> 00:03:56.166
<b>From his view, forgiving</b>
<b>enemies shows a big heart,</b>

57
00:03:56.166 --> 00:04:00.833
<b>and I've called his heart weak.</b>

58
00:04:00.833 --> 00:04:02.458
<b>He thinks</b>

59
00:04:02.458 --> 00:04:05.958
<b>I've lost faith in his abilities</b>

60
00:04:05.958 --> 00:04:13.375
<b>and that's why I'm opposing him.</b>

61
00:04:13.375 --> 00:04:22.000
<b>How could I doubt my dear Arjuna?</b>

62
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:28.875
<b>The real question isn't whether</b>
<b>I'm misunderstanding Arjuna.</b>

63
00:04:28.875 --> 00:04:32.875
<b>The real question is,</b>

64
00:04:32.875 --> 00:04:38.291
<b>does Arjuna truly understand himself?</b>

65
00:04:38.291 --> 00:04:43.750
<b>How others see us is never important.</b>

66
00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:45.541
<b>What matters is</b>

67
00:04:45.541 --> 00:04:50.125
<b>how well we understand ourselves.</b>

68
00:04:50.125 --> 00:04:51.875
<b>Agitation</b>

69
00:04:51.875 --> 00:04:53.375
<b>and anger</b>

70
00:04:53.375 --> 00:05:02.375
<b>are the signs that deep down, you know</b>
<b>you're not thinking right. </b>

71
00:05:02.375 --> 00:05:08.583
<b>Somewhere, you feel the</b>
<b>other person might be correct.</b>

72
00:05:08.583 --> 00:05:15.291
<b>Because if you were 100% sure of your thoughts,</b>

73
00:05:15.291 --> 00:05:21.083
<b>your mind would never waver,</b>

74
00:05:21.083 --> 00:05:24.666
<b>your responses wouldn't be heated or angry.</b>

75
00:05:24.666 --> 00:05:28.958
<b>You'd be calm.</b>

76
00:05:28.958 --> 00:05:32.416
<b>Pay attention to these signs.</b>

77
00:05:32.416 --> 00:05:37.625
<b>If someone's words agitate you,</b>

78
00:05:37.625 --> 00:05:48.541
<b>it's a sign you haven't</b>
<b>fully understood yourself yet.</b>

79
00:05:48.541 --> 00:05:54.708
<b>A pond's water is usually calm and gentle.</b>

80
00:05:54.708 --> 00:05:59.083
<b>You can see your reflection clearly.</b>

81
00:05:59.083 --> 00:06:00.500
<b>But</b>

82
00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:03.875
<b>throw in a pebble,</b>

83
00:06:03.875 --> 00:06:06.500
<b>and ripples form,</b>

84
00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:12.416
<b>making it hard to see yourself.</b>

85
00:06:12.416 --> 00:06:14.333
<b>Similarly,</b>

86
00:06:14.333 --> 00:06:15.791
<b>when the mind is calm,</b>

87
00:06:15.791 --> 00:06:19.708
<b>you can see yourself clearly.</b>

88
00:06:19.708 --> 00:06:20.875
<b>But if</b>

89
00:06:20.875 --> 00:06:26.458
<b>someone's words, like pebbles,</b>
<b>start disturbing you,</b>

90
00:06:26.458 --> 00:06:34.125
<b>you'll never see or know yourself properly.</b>

91
00:06:34.125 --> 00:06:40.541
<b>Keep an eye out for these signs of</b>
<b>agitation and anger.</b>

92
00:06:41.083 --> 00:06:45.291


93
00:06:45.291 --> 00:06:47.541
<b>Tradition says</b>

94
00:06:47.541 --> 00:06:53.208
<b>we should always respect</b>
<b>our elders in the family.</b>

95
00:06:53.208 --> 00:06:54.000
<b>But</b>

96
00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.583
<b>what if these elders stray</b>
<b>from the path of dharma?</b>

97
00:06:59.583 --> 00:07:04.500
<b>What if they lean towards</b>
<b>unrighteous behaviour? </b>

98
00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:05.583
<b>Or</b>

99
00:07:05.583 --> 00:07:08.791
<b> if they stand with the unrighteous,</b>

100
00:07:08.791 --> 00:07:11.083
<b>like grandsire Bhishma</b>

101
00:07:11.083 --> 00:07:13.333
<b> and guru Drona standing</b>

102
00:07:13.333 --> 00:07:16.625
<b>with the immoral Duryodhana?</b>

103
00:07:16.625 --> 00:07:20.458
<b>What should one do then?</b>

104
00:07:20.458 --> 00:07:25.416
<b>Arjuna believes that no matter what,</b>

105
00:07:25.416 --> 00:07:33.333
<b>respecting and honouring elders is always right.</b>

106
00:07:33.333 --> 00:07:37.666
<b>This view seems virtuous at first glance. </b>

107
00:07:37.666 --> 00:07:39.083
<b>But</b>

108
00:07:39.083 --> 00:07:47.416
<b>the true definition of dharma is far</b>
<b>above any individual or age. </b>

109
00:07:47.416 --> 00:07:50.375
<b>Dharma is about society</b>

110
00:07:50.375 --> 00:07:54.125
<b>and its impact on the society.</b>

111
00:07:54.125 --> 00:08:02.375
<b>Arjuna's idea of bowing before his relatives</b>
<b>by its effect on society right now, </b>

112
00:08:02.375 --> 00:08:07.500
<b>we should judge the righteousness of it.</b>

113
00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:12.875
<b>Arjuna abandoning the battle won't</b>
<b>raise the flag of dharma;</b>

114
00:08:12.875 --> 00:08:14.000
<b>it'll</b>

115
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:16.458
<b>only embolden the unrighteous forces,</b>

116
00:08:16.458 --> 00:08:19.458
<b> it'll embolden Duryodhana.</b>

117
00:08:19.458 --> 00:08:22.166
<b>If the Kauravas grow stronger,</b>

118
00:08:22.166 --> 00:08:24.708
<b>society will decline,</b>

119
00:08:24.708 --> 00:08:26.416
<b> not prosper.</b>

120
00:08:26.416 --> 00:08:27.833
<b>So in this case,</b>

121
00:08:27.833 --> 00:08:29.458
<b>putting dharma first,</b>

122
00:08:29.458 --> 00:08:33.875
<b>putting society's welfare first,</b>
<b>is the right thing to do.</b>

123
00:08:33.875 --> 00:08:35.250
<b>Respecting elders</b>

124
00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:38.000
<b>and following them is good,</b>

125
00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.166
<b>but if they've become unrighteous,</b>

126
00:08:41.166 --> 00:08:46.333
<b>following them becomes</b>
<b>unrighteous too, doesn't it?</b>

127
00:08:46.333 --> 00:08:48.416
<b>Dharma comes first,</b>

128
00:08:48.416 --> 00:08:50.958
<b>and whatever aligns with it is right.</b>

129
00:08:50.958 --> 00:08:55.416
<b>This is the best way to weigh right and wrong.</b>

130
00:08:55.958 --> 00:09:00.166


131
00:09:00.166 --> 00:09:04.166
<b>Arjuna's thinking is clouded,</b>

132
00:09:04.166 --> 00:09:05.250
<b>but</b>

133
00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:12.708
<b>this is just a part of mental and moral growth.</b>

134
00:09:12.708 --> 00:09:15.333
<b>Dark clouds gather,</b>

135
00:09:15.333 --> 00:09:18.666
<b>casting shadows everywhere,</b>

136
00:09:18.666 --> 00:09:19.500
<b>but</b>

137
00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:22.458
<b>their arrival is necessary.</b>

138
00:09:22.458 --> 00:09:29.791
<b>After all, they bring rain that greens</b>
<b>the earth, the trees, the plants,</b>

139
00:09:29.791 --> 00:09:32.000
<b>fills water bodies,</b>

140
00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:34.625
<b>and makes fields flourish.</b>

141
00:09:34.625 --> 00:09:36.541
<b>Similarly,</b>

142
00:09:36.541 --> 00:09:39.875
<b>the clouds in Arjuna's mind will part,</b>

143
00:09:39.875 --> 00:09:45.875
<b>and the rain of knowledge will make</b>
<b>his mind and intellect shine.</b>

144
00:09:45.875 --> 00:09:47.291
<b>But</b>

145
00:09:47.291 --> 00:09:52.500
<b>here's something you can learn from Arjuna</b>

146
00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:58.250
<b>he's not blindly accepting anything I say.</b>

147
00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:00.250
<b>He's arguing,</b>

148
00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:01.666
<b>questioning,</b>

149
00:10:01.666 --> 00:10:04.541
<b> presenting his viewpoint.</b>

150
00:10:04.541 --> 00:10:05.416
<b>Often, </b>

151
00:10:05.416 --> 00:10:11.958
<b>people become so dependent on a</b>
<b>particular person, a guru, or a well-wisher</b>

152
00:10:11.958 --> 00:10:17.125
<b>that they accept whatever they're told.</b>

153
00:10:17.125 --> 00:10:19.333
<b>But in doing so,</b>

154
00:10:19.333 --> 00:10:24.250
<b>they lose their life's independence.</b>

155
00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:26.250
<b>Arjuna is independent.</b>

156
00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:30.458
<b>He's not accepting things without thought.</b>

157
00:10:30.458 --> 00:10:32.166
<b>On the flip side,</b>

158
00:10:32.166 --> 00:10:35.375
<b>some become so rigid in their thinking</b>

159
00:10:35.375 --> 00:10:38.000
<b>that no matter how much a well-wisher explains,</b>

160
00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.916
<b>they stick to what their mind is set on.</b>

161
00:10:42.916 --> 00:10:46.375
<b>Such a person might seem self-reliant,</b>

162
00:10:46.375 --> 00:10:47.458
<b>but</b>

163
00:10:47.458 --> 00:10:54.416
<b>they're completely bound by</b>
<b>their ego and stubbornness.</b>

164
00:10:54.416 --> 00:10:58.833
<b>For progress in professional life,</b>
<b>both are necessary</b>

165
00:10:58.833 --> 00:11:01.750
<b>having independent thought</b>

166
00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:06.625
<b>and knowing when to</b>
<b>accept others' suggestions.</b>

167
00:11:06.625 --> 00:11:09.583
<b>Let’s think of it this way: </b>

168
00:11:09.583 --> 00:11:13.041
<b>when you go to the market to buy vegetables,</b>

169
00:11:13.041 --> 00:11:15.291
<b>you pick them up,</b>

170
00:11:15.291 --> 00:11:16.583
<b>examine them,</b>

171
00:11:16.583 --> 00:11:17.666
<b>test them.</b>

172
00:11:17.666 --> 00:11:25.750
<b>You leave behind what doesn't seem right</b>
<b>and buy what looks good.</b>

173
00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:30.333
<b>You bring those home and consume them.</b>

174
00:11:30.333 --> 00:11:34.541
<b>Do the same with other</b>
<b>people's ideas and suggestions.</b>

175
00:11:34.541 --> 00:11:38.625
<b>Neither adopt every suggestion</b>
<b>nor reject them outright.</b>

176
00:11:38.625 --> 00:11:40.458
<b>Pick up the idea,</b>

177
00:11:40.458 --> 00:11:41.500
<b>examine it,</b>

178
00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:42.833
<b>test it.</b>

179
00:11:42.833 --> 00:11:45.916
<b>Accept what's worth taking,</b>

180
00:11:45.916 --> 00:11:54.041
<b>and respectfully leave behind what</b>
<b>doesn't align with your values.</b>

